<%- include("partials/header") %>

<section class="page-heading">
  <p class="eyebrow">API reference</p>
  <h1>Endpoints, status codes, and limits.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The service exposes a small client API for registration and route setup, a
    token endpoint, and two proxy entry points. The live demo is restricted to
    path-prefix routing.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Endpoints</p>
    <h2>Public API surface</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Method</th>
          <th>Path</th>
          <th>Auth</th>
          <th>Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="method get">GET</span></td>
          <td><code>/health</code></td>
          <td>None</td>
          <td>Liveness check for the web service.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="method post">POST</span></td>
          <td><code>/client/register</code></td>
          <td>None</td>
          <td>Register a client and receive an API key.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="method post">POST</span></td>
          <td><code>/client/:clientid/routes</code></td>
          <td><code>x-api-key</code></td>
          <td>Create a path-to-upstream route for that client.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="method post">POST</span></td>
          <td><code>/oauth/token</code></td>
          <td><code>x-api-key</code></td>
          <td>Exchange an API key for a 15-minute JWT.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="method get">GET</span></td>
          <td><code>/p/:companyname/*route</code></td>
          <td><code>Bearer</code></td>
          <td>Path-prefix proxy mode.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="method get">GET</span></td>
          <td><code>*.&lt;DOMAIN&gt;/*route</code></td>
          <td><code>Bearer</code></td>
          <td>Subdomain proxy mode when wildcard DNS is available.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Register</p>
    <h2>Create a client</h2>
    <p>Registration is open but rate-limited per IP address.</p>
  </div>
  <pre class="code"><code>curl -X POST http://localhost:3000/client/register \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"companyName":"acme"}'</code></pre>
  <p class="response-label">Response</p>
  <pre class="code"><code>{
  "success": true,
  "message": "Registration Successfull",
  "client": [
    {
      "id": "uuid",
      "companyName": "acme",
      "apiKey": "auth_sec_..."
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Routes</p>
    <h2>Map a path to an upstream</h2>
    <p>
      Route creation requires the API key that belongs to the client. The
      public demo only accepts the two built-in dummy targets.
    </p>
  </div>
  <pre class="code"><code>curl -X POST http://localhost:3000/client/&lt;client-id&gt;/routes \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "x-api-key: auth_sec_&lt;your-api-key&gt;" \
  -d '{"path":"orders","targeturl":"http://127.0.0.1:3001"}'</code></pre>
  <p class="response-label">Response</p>
  <pre class="code"><code>{
  "success": true,
  "message": "Route created successfully",
  "client_route": true
}</code></pre>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Tokens</p>
    <h2>Exchange an API key for a JWT</h2>
  </div>
  <pre class="code"><code>curl -X POST http://localhost:3000/oauth/token \
  -H "x-api-key: auth_sec_&lt;your-api-key&gt;"</code></pre>
  <p class="response-label">Response</p>
  <pre class="code"><code>{
  "success": true,
  "token": "&lt;jwt&gt;"
}</code></pre>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Proxy</p>
    <h2>Call the gateway</h2>
    <p>
      Register the route <code>orders</code> first, then request a path beneath
      it. The JWT from the previous step goes in the authorization header.
    </p>
  </div>
  <pre class="code"><code>curl http://localhost:3000/p/acme/orders/123 \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer &lt;access-token&gt;"</code></pre>
</section>

<section class="section" id="subdomain-routing">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Routing modes</p>
    <h2>Subdomain routing on Render</h2>
    <p>
      Subdomain mode needs wildcard DNS such as <code>*.example.com</code>
      pointing at the service. On <code>*.onrender.com</code> you do not control
      wildcard subdomains, so the hosted demo supports only path-prefix mode:
    </p>
  </div>
  <pre class="code"><code># Works on the hosted demo
curl http://localhost:3000/p/acme/orders/123 \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer &lt;access-token&gt;"

# Needs wildcard DNS for DOMAIN; not available on onrender.com
curl http://acme.example.com:3000/orders/123 \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer &lt;access-token&gt;"</code></pre>
  <p>
    The EJS pages live at <code>/</code>, <code>/try</code>, and
    <code>/docs</code>. They are registered before the
    <code>/p/:companyname/*route</code> handler and skipped for the service's
    own hostname, so they never collide with the proxy or the vhost
    middleware.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Errors</p>
    <h2>Status codes</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Status</th>
          <th>Meaning</th>
          <th>Typical cause</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="status ok">200</span></td>
          <td>Success</td>
          <td>Registration, route creation, token issued, or proxied response.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="status warn">400</span></td>
          <td>Bad request</td>
          <td>Missing fields, invalid target URL, or a disallowed demo target.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="status warn">401</span></td>
          <td>Unauthorized</td>
          <td>Missing or invalid JWT, missing route, or wrong <code>x-api-key</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="status warn">403</span></td>
          <td>Forbidden target</td>
          <td>The saved route does not point at an allowed upstream.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="status warn">404</span></td>
          <td>Not found</td>
          <td>Unknown client or route, or an unknown API path.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="status warn">429</span></td>
          <td>Rate limited</td>
          <td>More than 100 proxy requests per minute, or too many registrations.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="status bad">502</span></td>
          <td>Bad gateway</td>
          <td>The upstream server could not be reached.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Limits</p>
    <h2>Rate limiting</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <article class="card">
      <h3>Proxy calls</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>100 requests per minute</strong> per client route. Counters live
        in Redis and reset after 60 seconds.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>Client registration</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>5 registrations per 15 minutes</strong> per IP address. A
        <code>429</code> includes a <code>Retry-After</code> header.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Security</p>
    <h2>Public demo safeguards</h2>
  </div>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Demo mode only accepts the two built-in dummy backend target URLs.</li>
    <li>
      Route creation requires the API key belonging to the specified client.
    </li>
    <li>
      With demo mode disabled, target URLs are checked against private,
      loopback, link-local, and reserved address ranges before they are saved.
    </li>
    <li>
      The proxy re-checks the saved target on every request, so old database
      rows cannot bypass the allowlist.
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<%- include("partials/footer") %>
